# SYNOPSIS

```usage
find [-H | -L] path ... [expression ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`find` walks the tree below each <path>, the <path> included, and evaluates the <expression> for each file it meets, with the file's name as the <path> it was reached by followed by the names below it.
The <path>s are the arguments before the first that begins with `-`, or is `!` or `(`.
With no <expression>, or one with no `-exec`, `-ok`, `-print` or `-print0`, each file for which the <expression> is true is printed, one name to a line.

: `-H`
  Follow symbolic links named as <path>s.
: `-L`
  Follow every symbolic link; a link that leads nowhere is taken as itself.

An <expression> is primaries joined by operators.
A primary that takes a number <n> takes `+`<n> for more than <n>, `-`<n> for fewer, and <n> for exactly <n>.
The primaries are:

: `-name` <pattern>
  The last component of the name matches the shell pattern <pattern>, as `fnmatch` matches; a leading `.` is matched by a wildcard.
: `-path` <pattern>
  The whole name matches <pattern>; a `/` is matched by a wildcard.
: `-type` <c>
  The file is of type <c>: `b` block device, `c` character device, `d` directory, `l` symbolic link, `p` FIFO, `f` regular file or `s` socket.
: `-perm` <mode>, `-perm -`<mode>
  The file's permission bits are exactly <mode>, octal or symbolic as chmod(1) takes it; with `-`, at least the bits of <mode> are set.
: `-links` <n>
  The file has <n> links.
: `-size` <n>, `-size` <n>`c`
  The file's size, rounded up to 512-byte blocks, is <n> blocks; with `c`, it is <n> bytes.
: `-atime` <n>, `-ctime` <n>, `-mtime` <n>
  The file was last accessed, changed, or modified <n> whole days of 86400 seconds before `find` started.
: `-newer` <file>
  The file was modified later than <file>, to the second.
: `-user` <name>, `-group` <name>
  The file is owned by the user or group <name>, or by the number <name>.
: `-nouser`, `-nogroup`
  The file's owner, or group, has no name in the user database.
: `-print`
  Always true; print the name and a newline.
: `-print0`
  Always true; print the name and a NUL byte.
: `-exec` <cmd> ... `;`
  Run <cmd> with its arguments, an argument `{}` replaced by the name, and wait for it; true if it exits 0.
: `-exec` <cmd> ... `{}` `+`
  Always true; gather names, and run <cmd> with as many of them at the end of its arguments as fit, as often as needed, the last time when `find` finishes.
  `{}` must be the argument just before `+`.
  `find` exits 1 if any run of <cmd> fails.
: `-ok` <cmd> ... `;`
  As `-exec` with `;`, but first ask on standard error, as `cmd: name ? `, and run <cmd> only if the line read from standard input begins with `y` or `Y`.
: `-prune`
  Always true; do not descend into the file if it is a directory.
: `-depth`
  Always true; evaluate each directory's contents before the directory itself, for the whole walk.
: `-xdev`
  Always true; do not descend into directories on another file system than the <path> they are below.

The operators, from the tightest binding to the loosest, are `(` <expression> `)`; `!` <expression>, true if <expression> is false; <expression> `-a` <expression>, true if both are, also written with no operator between them; and <expression> `-o` <expression>, true if either is.
The second operand of `-a` and `-o` is evaluated only when the first does not decide.
The shell's special characters among them, and `;`, must be quoted.

`find` is sbase's, and found only in the POSIX namespace: `/boot/bin/posix/find` is a hard link to `sbase-box`, which runs the command its name names, and `/lib/ns/posix` (a manifest's `ns=posix`, see namespace(6)) binds `/boot/bin/posix` before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program that runs `find` finds it there.
There is no native `find`.

# EXAMPLES

`tests/posix/sbasetest.c` runs `find /tmp/sb -type d -name sub` in the POSIX namespace, in a tree it has made with the files `/tmp/sb/f` and `/tmp/sb/h` and the directory `/tmp/sb/sub`, and checks that it prints `/tmp/sb/sub` and exits 0.

